begin;

-- Track A depth repair for the published Form 4 History & Government corpus.
-- Adds evidence, causation, comparison and judgement blocks while preserving
-- every pre-existing content block. Idempotence is keyed by the first block id
-- of each chapter's repair pack.

with target as (
  select id from public.vibe_publications
  where title='Vibe History & Government Form 4' and status='published'
  order by published_at desc nulls last limit 1
), packs(number, sentinel, additions) as (
  values
  (1,'ww1-depth-crisis-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww1-depth-crisis-h','type','heading2','content','Why the July Crisis became a general war','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww1-depth-crisis-p','type','paragraph','content','The assassination at Sarajevo did not mechanically cause a world war. What made the July Crisis so dangerous was the interaction between alliance commitments, military planning and political choices. Austria-Hungary wanted to weaken Serbia and secured strong German backing before issuing a deliberately severe ultimatum. Serbia accepted many demands but rejected provisions that would have compromised its sovereignty. Russia, presenting itself as a protector of Serbia and seeking influence in the Balkans, moved toward mobilisation. German leaders interpreted Russian mobilisation through war plans that assumed Germany would have to defeat France quickly before turning east. Germany therefore declared war on Russia and France and invaded neutral Belgium. Britain entered after the violation of Belgian neutrality and because a German-dominated western Europe threatened British strategic interests. The sequence shows why historians distinguish background causes from escalation. Alliances did not force governments to fight automatically; leaders made decisions within an international system in which delay, mobilisation and fear of strategic disadvantage narrowed the space for compromise.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww1-depth-eastafrica-h','type','heading2','content','The war in East Africa and its human cost','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww1-depth-eastafrica-p','type','paragraph','content','For learners in Kenya, the East African campaign is essential because the First World War was not only fought in European trenches. British forces and their allies campaigned against German East Africa, while large numbers of Africans served as soldiers, porters and carriers. The Carrier Corps moved food, ammunition and equipment across difficult terrain where roads and railways were limited. Disease, exhaustion, inadequate supplies and displacement made the campaign extremely costly for African communities. Livestock, food and labour could be requisitioned for the war effort, while trade and normal production were disrupted. The campaign also demonstrates the unequal structure of colonial warfare: European empires made strategic decisions, but African populations supplied much of the labour and endured major social and economic costs. In later political memory, wartime service, taxation, labour demands and exposure to wider regions contributed to changing expectations, although anti-colonial nationalism developed through many causes and should not be reduced to wartime experience alone.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect','context','Kenya and East Africa')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww1-depth-peace-h','type','heading2','content','Peace settlement, Versailles and the unstable post-war order','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww1-depth-peace-p','type','paragraph','content','The Armistice of 11 November 1918 ended the fighting, but the peace settlement created new disputes. At Paris, the victorious powers pursued different goals. France emphasised security against another German attack; Britain sought a workable European balance and protection of imperial interests; the United States promoted principles associated with President Woodrow Wilson, including a new international organisation. The Treaty of Versailles reduced German military power, transferred territory, imposed reparations obligations and included the war-guilt clause. Other treaties dismantled the Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman imperial orders and recognised or reshaped states in central and eastern Europe. The settlement promoted national self-determination selectively: some European peoples gained states, while colonial peoples did not receive equivalent independence. Former German colonies became League mandates administered by victorious powers. This contradiction matters for African history. The post-war order spoke the language of international responsibility while preserving imperial rule. Resentment over the settlement, economic instability and unresolved national questions later became part of the background to the Second World War, though Versailles alone cannot explain the conflict of 1939.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend'))
  )),
  (2,'league-depth-security-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','league-depth-security-h','type','heading2','content','How collective security was supposed to work','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','league-depth-security-p','type','paragraph','content','Collective security rested on a simple but demanding principle: aggression against one member should concern all members. Instead of every state relying only on its own military alliances, disputes were to be discussed, investigated and, where possible, settled peacefully. If a state used aggression, members could apply diplomatic and economic pressure and, in principle, support stronger collective measures. This system required governments to accept short-term costs for the wider peace. That was its central weakness. The League had no independent army and depended on member states to enforce decisions. Major powers also differed over what risks they were willing to take. Britain and France carried much of the burden after the United States remained outside the organisation, while Germany and the Soviet Union had changing relationships with it. Collective security therefore worked best when disputes involved smaller states and the major powers broadly agreed; it was much weaker when action threatened the interests of a powerful aggressor or required serious economic and military sacrifice.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','league-depth-cases-h','type','heading2','content','Success and failure through case studies','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','league-depth-cases-p','type','paragraph','content','The League achieved useful successes in the 1920s. In the Åland Islands dispute between Sweden and Finland, both sides accepted League involvement and the settlement left the islands under Finnish sovereignty while protecting their cultural identity. In the Upper Silesia dispute between Germany and Poland, international supervision and negotiation helped manage a difficult territorial question. The League also undertook humanitarian work involving refugees, health, labour and campaigns against trafficking. These achievements show that the organisation was not simply useless. The deeper test came when aggressive great powers rejected restraint. After Japan occupied Manchuria in 1931, the League investigated but acted slowly and failed to reverse the aggression; Japan later left the League. In 1935 Fascist Italy invaded Ethiopia. Sanctions were incomplete and did not include decisive measures capable of stopping the invasion, while Britain and France feared driving Mussolini closer to Hitler. The failure was particularly damaging because Ethiopia was an independent African member state. The contrast between smaller disputes and major-power aggression reveals the conditions under which collective security succeeded or failed.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','league-depth-judgement-h','type','heading2','content','Why the League failed: a balanced judgement','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','league-depth-judgement-p','type','paragraph','content','A strong KCSE answer should avoid treating one weakness as the complete explanation. The absence of the United States reduced the League’s authority and resources, but membership alone would not have solved every crisis. The unanimity rule and slow decision-making could delay action. The League lacked its own armed force. Economic sanctions worked only if major trading states cooperated. Britain and France were weakened by the First World War and often placed national security and imperial interests ahead of collective action. The Great Depression increased economic nationalism and political extremism. Aggressive states such as Japan, Italy and Nazi Germany were increasingly prepared to defy international opinion. Most importantly, collective security required member governments to act together before aggression succeeded. In Manchuria and Ethiopia, that political will was absent. The best judgement therefore combines institutional weaknesses, the behaviour of aggressor states and the reluctance of major powers to bear the costs of enforcement.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend'))
  )),
  (3,'ww2-depth-road-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww2-depth-road-h','type','heading2','content','From Nazi consolidation to war in 1939','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww2-depth-road-p','type','paragraph','content','The road to war developed through a series of tests of the post-1919 settlement. After taking power in 1933, Hitler withdrew Germany from the League of Nations and pursued rearmament in defiance of Versailles. Germany reintroduced conscription and rebuilt its armed forces. In 1936 German troops remilitarised the Rhineland, a major gamble that met no military resistance from Britain or France. Hitler then strengthened Germany’s position through the Rome-Berlin Axis and closer relations with Italy. In March 1938 Germany annexed Austria in the Anschluss. Later that year the Munich Agreement transferred the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia to Germany after Britain and France followed a policy of appeasement. When German forces occupied the rest of the Czech lands in March 1939, it became harder to argue that Hitler only wanted to unite German-speaking peoples. Britain and France guaranteed Poland. The Nazi-Soviet Pact of August 1939 reduced Hitler’s immediate fear of a two-front war and secretly divided spheres of influence in eastern Europe. Germany invaded Poland on 1 September; Britain and France declared war two days later.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww2-depth-turning-h','type','heading2','content','Why the major turning points mattered','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww2-depth-turning-p','type','paragraph','content','The course of the war changed because the Axis powers failed to convert early victories into a sustainable global advantage. Germany’s rapid campaigns defeated Poland and much of western Europe, but Britain remained in the war after the Battle of Britain prevented German air superiority for an invasion. Operation Barbarossa opened an enormous eastern front in June 1941. German forces advanced deeply into the Soviet Union but faced distance, supply problems, fierce resistance and the failure to achieve a quick victory. The battle of Stalingrad in 1942–1943 destroyed a major German army and shifted strategic momentum in the east. In North Africa, the Allied victory at El Alamein and the Anglo-American landings in Operation Torch squeezed Axis forces from both directions. In the Pacific, Japan’s attack on Pearl Harbor brought the United States fully into the war; the Battle of Midway in June 1942 severely weakened Japanese carrier strength. By 1944 the Allies possessed growing industrial, manpower and logistical advantages. The D-Day landings opened a major western front, while Soviet armies advanced from the east. These turning points mattered because they changed resources, initiative and the number of fronts the Axis had to defend.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww2-depth-africa-h','type','heading2','content','Africa, Kenya and the Second World War','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww2-depth-africa-p','type','paragraph','content','The Second World War again mobilised African territories for an imperial conflict. African troops served in campaigns in East Africa, North Africa, the Middle East and Asia. Men from Kenya served in formations such as the King’s African Rifles, while colonial economies supplied food, labour and strategic materials. Wartime demand could create new employment and production but also produced shortages, price pressures and tighter state controls. Military service exposed many Africans to new places, skills and political ideas, while the contradiction between fighting fascism abroad and remaining under colonial rule became increasingly visible. After 1945, veterans returned to societies where land, wages and political representation remained contested. The war did not cause African nationalism by itself, but it accelerated changes already under way: European powers were economically weakened, international discussion of self-determination became harder to ignore, and organised African political movements became more assertive. For Kenya, these developments formed part of the wider background to intensified post-war nationalism and the struggle against colonial rule.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect','context','Kenya and Africa')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww2-depth-order-h','type','heading2','content','The post-war order and the meaning of victory','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ww2-depth-order-p','type','paragraph','content','The defeat of the Axis powers transformed international politics. Germany was occupied and divided, Japan was occupied and demilitarised, and the United Nations was created in 1945 with a Security Council designed to give the major victorious powers a central role in maintaining peace. The United States and Soviet Union emerged as superpowers, and their wartime alliance soon gave way to Cold War rivalry. European colonial powers survived the war but were economically and politically weakened, while independence movements grew stronger across Asia and Africa. The Holocaust and other wartime atrocities also shaped new international debates about human rights and crimes against humanity. Economically, reconstruction required enormous resources and encouraged new forms of international cooperation. A strong historical judgement therefore treats 1945 not merely as the end of fighting but as a transition to a different world order: one shaped by the UN, superpower rivalry, decolonisation and attempts to prevent another global catastrophe.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend'))
  )),
  (4,'ir-deeper-un-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','ir-deeper-un-h','type','heading2','content','The UN: power, legitimacy and limits','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ir-deeper-un-p','type','paragraph','content','The United Nations was designed to avoid some weaknesses of the League of Nations, but its structure also reflects the realities of power. The General Assembly gives all member states a forum and one vote each, while the Security Council carries primary responsibility for international peace and security. Five permanent members possess veto power, meaning that enforcement can be blocked when a major power believes its vital interests are threatened. This arrangement was intended to keep the strongest states inside the organisation rather than outside it, but it also limits equal decision-making. UN agencies and programmes have contributed to health, education, refugees, food security and development, while peacekeeping has helped contain some conflicts. Yet the UN is not a world government: it depends on member states for troops, finance, sanctions and political support. Its effectiveness therefore varies from case to case. A balanced answer should distinguish the organisation’s legal authority and humanitarian achievements from the political constraints created by sovereignty and great-power rivalry.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ir-deeper-nam-h','type','heading2','content','Non-alignment, Africa and Kenya','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ir-deeper-nam-p','type','paragraph','content','The Non-Aligned Movement emerged in a world divided by the Cold War but populated increasingly by newly independent states. Non-alignment did not always mean complete neutrality. It meant defending the right of states to make foreign-policy choices without becoming subordinate to either the United States-led or Soviet-led bloc. Leaders associated with the movement included Jawaharlal Nehru of India, Josip Broz Tito of Yugoslavia, Gamal Abdel Nasser of Egypt, Kwame Nkrumah of Ghana and Sukarno of Indonesia. African states used non-aligned diplomacy to support decolonisation, oppose racial rule and press for a greater voice in international institutions. Kenya’s foreign policy after independence combined African cooperation, Commonwealth ties, economic relationships with Western states and participation in multilateral organisations. For examination purposes, the important point is that newly independent states were not passive objects of Cold War rivalry: they also pursued their own security, development and diplomatic interests.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect','context','Kenya, Africa and non-alignment'))
  )),
  (5,'ca-deeper-liberation-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','ca-deeper-liberation-h','type','heading2','content','Continental cooperation and the liberation struggle','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ca-deeper-liberation-p','type','paragraph','content','One of the OAU’s most important historical roles was support for the completion of African decolonisation. Its Liberation Committee assisted movements opposing Portuguese colonial rule and minority regimes in southern Africa. Independent African states provided diplomatic support, training bases, material assistance or political pressure in different ways. The organisation helped keep the independence of Angola, Mozambique, Guinea-Bissau, Namibia and the struggle against apartheid on the continental and international agenda. At the same time, member governments disagreed over strategy and ideology. Some favoured rapid continental political union, while others preferred to protect the sovereignty of newly independent states and build cooperation gradually. This tension between unity and sovereignty shaped the OAU from its birth. It explains both its achievement in creating a common African forum and its caution when dealing with conflicts inside member states.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ca-deeper-integration-h','type','heading2','content','Why regional integration is difficult','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ca-deeper-integration-p','type','paragraph','content','Regional integration promises larger markets, coordinated infrastructure and greater bargaining power, but those benefits are not automatic. Member states may produce similar goods and compete for the same investments. More industrialised economies can gain faster from an enlarged market, creating fears of unequal benefit. Governments also depend on customs revenue and may hesitate to remove trade barriers. Different currencies, regulations, transport systems and political priorities increase the cost of coordination. Overlapping membership in several regional organisations can create conflicting obligations. The history of the East African Community illustrates both possibilities and risks: earlier cooperation created important shared institutions, but disagreements over political relations, financing and distribution of benefits contributed to the collapse of the first Community in 1977. The revival of the EAC demonstrates that failed integration can be rebuilt when states judge that cooperation again serves their interests. A strong answer therefore explains both economic incentives and political conditions.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ca-deeper-judgement-h','type','heading2','content','How to judge African cooperation historically','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','ca-deeper-judgement-p','type','paragraph','content','When asked whether African cooperation has succeeded, avoid a simple yes-or-no conclusion. Use criteria. In liberation, the OAU helped coordinate diplomatic and material support and defended the principle of African independence. In conflict management, the record was more uneven because non-interference limited action and member states sometimes backed opposing sides. In economic integration, organisations have reduced some barriers and built common institutions, but implementation is slower than treaty promises. In political unity, Africa remains a continent of sovereign states rather than a federation, yet continental organisations have created regular mechanisms for negotiation and common positions. The strongest judgement recognises that cooperation is a process. Institutions can matter even when they do not achieve every objective: they create forums, rules and expectations that make some forms of collective action easier than they would be without them.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend'))
  )),
  (6,'np-deeper-nation-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','np-deeper-nation-h','type','heading2','content','National philosophies as tools of nation-building','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','np-deeper-nation-p','type','paragraph','content','National philosophies were political programmes as well as economic ideas. New African states inherited borders created under colonial rule, populations with different historical identities and public institutions designed primarily for colonial administration. Leaders therefore needed a language that could encourage loyalty to the new nation and define the responsibilities of citizens. In Kenya, Harambee emphasised collective effort and became associated with community development. In Tanzania, Ujamaa and the promotion of Kiswahili were linked to a strong project of national integration. In Zambia, Humanism presented the person and community as the moral centre of public policy. These philosophies helped governments explain what independence was supposed to achieve. However, nation-building could also be used to justify concentration of political authority or to discourage opposition. The historian must therefore examine both the inclusive ideals expressed by a philosophy and the political system in which it was implemented.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','np-deeper-economics-h','type','heading2','content','Economic constraints on post-independence philosophies','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','np-deeper-economics-p','type','paragraph','content','African governments did not implement their philosophies in an economic vacuum. Many states inherited economies dependent on a small number of exports, imported manufactured goods, limited local capital and major inequalities in land, education and infrastructure. World commodity prices could sharply affect government revenue. Drought, oil-price shocks and debt also constrained policy. Kenya’s mixed-economy approach attracted private investment and supported growth in some sectors, but did not remove inequalities. Tanzania’s stronger state-led programme expanded access to some social services but struggled with productivity, foreign exchange and the costs of villagisation. Zambia’s dependence on copper made national plans especially vulnerable when copper prices fell. These examples show why examination answers should not attribute every success or failure to ideology alone. Policy design, administrative capacity, international conditions and the inherited structure of the economy all shaped outcomes.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','np-deeper-evidence-h','type','heading2','content','Evidence and judgement in KCSE answers','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','np-deeper-evidence-p','type','paragraph','content','A high-quality answer on national philosophies should move in four steps: identify the philosophy, explain the problem it addressed, describe how it was implemented, and evaluate results using evidence. For Harambee, do not stop at “people pulled together”; explain community contributions to schools, health facilities or water projects and then note limitations such as unequal local capacity. For Ujamaa, connect equality and self-reliance to the Arusha Declaration, public ownership and villagisation, then assess both social-service goals and production problems. For African Socialism, connect the mixed economy and development planning to Sessional Paper No. 10 of 1965 and evaluate both growth and distribution. Comparison becomes stronger when the same criteria are used for each case. This turns a list of slogans into historical analysis.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend'))
  )),
  (7,'dk-deeper-state-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','dk-deeper-state-h','type','heading2','content','Building the post-colonial Kenyan state','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','dk-deeper-state-p','type','paragraph','content','Independence transferred political authority to an African-led government, but the new state inherited institutions, regional inequalities and economic structures created during colonial rule. Kenya became a republic in 1964, replacing the British monarch as head of state with a president. Political competition narrowed over time and Kenya became a de facto one-party state before KANU became the only legal party in 1982. Domestic pressure, international change and demands for political reform contributed to restoration of multiparty politics in 1991. The 1990s and 2000s brought competitive elections but also serious disputes over governance, constitutional reform and electoral integrity. The Constitution of 2010 restructured institutions by strengthening the separation of powers, creating a Supreme Court, establishing county governments and expanding the constitutional framework for rights and independent commissions. These changes should be understood as part of a long process of state-building rather than isolated dates.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','dk-deeper-land-h','type','heading2','content','Land, growth and inequality after independence','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','dk-deeper-land-p','type','paragraph','content','Economic development after independence combined important growth with persistent inequality. Agriculture remained central, while tourism, manufacturing, finance, transport and services expanded. Africanisation increased African participation in business and public administration, and government investment extended roads, schools, health facilities and other infrastructure. Yet land remained politically sensitive because colonial dispossession and unequal ownership had not disappeared at independence. Settlement schemes redistributed some former settler land, but access to productive land and credit remained uneven. Rapid population growth increased pressure on farms and urban employment. Regional differences in infrastructure and public investment also shaped opportunity. A good historical explanation therefore distinguishes aggregate growth from distribution: an economy can expand while particular regions, classes or communities remain disadvantaged. This distinction helps explain why debates about poverty, unemployment, corruption, land and regional inequality continued despite visible national development.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','dk-deeper-social-h','type','heading2','content','Social transformation and the pressures of modernisation','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','dk-deeper-social-p','type','paragraph','content','Kenya experienced major social change after 1963. School enrolment expanded and universities and technical institutions grew, creating a larger educated workforce and new expectations of social mobility. Health services expanded, contributing with other factors to improvements in survival, although access remained unequal between places and income groups. Urbanisation accelerated as Nairobi, Mombasa and other towns attracted migrants seeking employment and services. Rapid urban growth produced housing shortages, informal settlements, congestion and pressure on water, sanitation and transport. Population growth also increased demand for schools, land and jobs. At the same time, media, transport and communication linked communities more closely and helped create a more nationally connected society. These developments show why social progress and social problems often occur together: expanding education can raise opportunity while also increasing demand for jobs; urban growth can support economic activity while creating new planning challenges.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect','context','Kenya since independence'))
  )),
  (8,'da-deeper-comparison-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','da-deeper-comparison-h','type','heading2','content','Why African post-independence experiences diverged','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','da-deeper-comparison-p','type','paragraph','content','African states inherited different colonial institutions, resource bases and political movements, so independence produced varied paths. Ghana inherited an economy highly dependent on cocoa and pursued ambitious state-led development under Kwame Nkrumah before experiencing military intervention. Tanzania under Julius Nyerere emphasised Ujamaa, rural transformation and national integration. Nigeria combined great oil wealth with regional and ethnic tensions, military rule and a devastating civil war from 1967 to 1970. The Democratic Republic of Congo entered independence with weak national institutions, secessionist crises and intense Cold War intervention. Botswana, by contrast, developed relatively stable electoral institutions while using diamond revenue to expand state capacity and public services. These differences warn against a single narrative of “African failure” or “African success.” Historical explanation must connect outcomes to leadership choices, colonial legacies, economic structures, international pressures and the strength of institutions in particular states.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','da-deeper-economy-h','type','heading2','content','Debt, structural adjustment and economic reform','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','da-deeper-economy-p','type','paragraph','content','By the late twentieth century many African governments faced severe fiscal and balance-of-payments pressures. Export earnings could fall when commodity prices declined, while oil-price shocks, drought, expensive development projects and rising external debt increased pressure on public budgets. International lenders often supported structural adjustment programmes that called for currency reform, reduced subsidies, privatisation, trade liberalisation and tighter public spending. Supporters argued that these measures could correct inefficient state intervention and restore economic stability. Critics pointed to job losses, reduced access to subsidised services and the social cost of rapid adjustment. Outcomes varied across countries because starting conditions and implementation differed. For KCSE analysis, structural adjustment should be treated as part of a broader economic history: it interacted with inherited export dependence, domestic policy choices, debt, global markets and the administrative capacity of the state.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','da-deeper-judgement-h','type','heading2','content','How to write a balanced Africa-since-independence judgement','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','da-deeper-judgement-p','type','paragraph','content','A strong answer should pair challenge with achievement. Political instability, coups and civil wars affected several states, but many countries also built durable national institutions and later expanded competitive politics. Dependence on primary exports remained serious, yet African economies also diversified into services, manufacturing, telecommunications and regional trade to different degrees. Education and health expanded substantially from the very low levels inherited at independence, even as population growth and unequal resources kept pressure on services. African states also increased cooperation through regional organisations and the African Union. The best conclusion therefore avoids measuring the whole continent by a single country or decade. Use specific cases and explain change over time. This approach demonstrates historical reasoning rather than repeating general statements about Africa.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend'))
  )),
  (9,'la-deeper-transition-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','la-deeper-transition-h','type','heading2','content','From local authorities to constitutional devolution','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','la-deeper-transition-p','type','paragraph','content','The former local-authority system and the county system should not be treated as the same institution with different names. Local authorities operated mainly under ordinary legislation and included municipal, county, town and urban councils with powers assigned by national law. Their resources and administrative capacity varied considerably, and national government retained strong control over the system. The Constitution of Kenya 2010 instead establishes national and county government as distinct but interdependent levels, assigns functions constitutionally and protects a system of revenue sharing. Forty-seven counties have elected governors and county assemblies. This gives devolution a stronger constitutional foundation than the earlier local-authority framework. The change was intended to bring services and decision-making closer to citizens, reduce over-centralisation, promote more equitable development and increase participation. In examination answers, learners should compare legal foundation, leadership, functions, finance and accountability rather than merely listing county offices.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','la-deeper-account-h','type','heading2','content','Participation, accountability and the limits of devolution','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','la-deeper-account-p','type','paragraph','content','Devolution creates opportunities for citizens to influence planning and monitor services closer to where they live. County assemblies legislate and exercise oversight, while public participation is expected in planning and budgeting processes. Counties receive nationally shared revenue and may raise own-source revenue within the law. These arrangements can make local priorities more visible, but institutions do not guarantee good outcomes automatically. Counties can face weak revenue collection, procurement problems, corruption, political conflict, shortages of technical staff and disputes over functions or resources. Unequal economic bases also mean counties begin with different capacities. Historical and civic analysis should therefore distinguish the objectives of devolution from its implementation. A useful judgement asks whether services, accountability, participation and regional equity have improved, what evidence supports that conclusion, and which challenges arise from institutional design versus administrative practice.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect','context','Kenyan county government')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','la-deeper-exam-h','type','heading2','content','Building comparison in a KCSE answer','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','la-deeper-exam-p','type','paragraph','content','When comparing former local authorities with devolved county governments, organise the answer by criteria. Under legal basis, contrast statutory local authorities with constitutionally established counties. Under leadership, identify the structures through which each system was governed. Under functions, explain what services or responsibilities were assigned. Under finance, distinguish local revenues and transfers and explain why reliable finance matters for service delivery. Under accountability, consider elected institutions, oversight and public participation. This structure prevents a common exam weakness: writing two separate lists without actually comparing them. Each paragraph should state the criterion, explain the difference and show why the difference matters.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend'))
  )),
  (10,'pf-deeper-principles-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','pf-deeper-principles-h','type','heading2','content','Principles of public finance in Kenya','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','pf-deeper-principles-p','type','paragraph','content','Public finance is not only about collecting taxes; it is also about legitimacy, distribution and accountability. Kenya’s constitutional public-finance framework emphasises openness, accountability, public participation, equitable sharing of public resources and prudent use of public money. These principles help explain why budgeting is a political and civic process as well as an accounting exercise. Revenue decisions distribute burdens: direct taxes are linked more closely to income or profits, while indirect taxes are paid through spending and can affect households differently. Expenditure decisions distribute benefits by determining which services, regions and programmes receive resources. Borrowing shifts some costs into the future and is therefore most defensible when it supports productive or long-lived investments and remains sustainable. In an examination answer, learners should connect each source of revenue or type of expenditure to its purpose and consequence rather than giving an unexplained list.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','connect','context','Kenyan public finance')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','pf-deeper-budget-h','type','heading2','content','From policy priorities to an accountable budget','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','pf-deeper-budget-p','type','paragraph','content','A budget translates government priorities into authorised revenue and expenditure. The process begins before money is spent: institutions estimate revenue, identify needs and prepare proposals within policy and legal frameworks. Legislatures scrutinise and authorise expenditure, after which public bodies implement programmes and procure goods and services. Accounting, internal controls, audit and legislative oversight are needed to compare authorised spending with what actually occurred. This cycle matters because a budget can fail even when its initial priorities are sensible. Revenue may fall below forecasts, projects may be delayed, procurement may be wasteful or funds may be diverted from intended purposes. Effective financial management therefore requires realistic planning, transparent execution and evidence of results. For learners, the key distinction is between allocation and outcome: assigning money to a programme is not the same as proving that the programme delivered the intended service.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','pf-deeper-debt-h','type','heading2','content','Public debt: useful tool or future burden?','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','pf-deeper-debt-p','type','paragraph','content','Borrowing allows government to finance expenditure when current revenue is insufficient, and it can be economically useful when it funds infrastructure or investment whose benefits last for many years. However, debt creates obligations for future budgets through interest and repayment. Heavy debt-service costs can reduce the resources available for health, education, maintenance and other priorities. Foreign-currency borrowing also creates exchange-rate risk because repayment becomes more expensive in domestic currency if the exchange rate weakens. A balanced historical or government answer should therefore reject both extremes: borrowing is neither automatically bad nor free money. Its value depends on why the funds are borrowed, the cost and terms, how effectively projects are implemented, the economy’s capacity to generate future revenue and whether debt remains sustainable.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend'))
  )),
  (11,'cg-deeper-britain-h',jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('id','cg-deeper-britain-h','type','heading2','content','Britain and the logic of parliamentary government','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','cg-deeper-britain-p','type','paragraph','content','Britain provides the classic example of a parliamentary system in which the executive is drawn from Parliament and remains politically dependent on the confidence of the House of Commons. The monarch is head of state, while the prime minister is head of government. The cabinet is normally formed from senior members of the governing party or coalition and is collectively responsible for government policy. Party discipline can make the executive powerful when it has a secure parliamentary majority, but ministers remain exposed to parliamentary questions, debate, committees and ultimately political removal. This illustrates an important comparative point: separation of powers is not absolute in a parliamentary system. Executive and legislative personnel overlap, and accountability depends heavily on parliamentary confidence, party competition, conventions, courts, elections and public scrutiny.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','cg-deeper-us-h','type','heading2','content','The United States and separated institutions','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','cg-deeper-us-p','type','paragraph','content','The United States illustrates presidential government with constitutionally separated institutions. The president is elected separately from Congress and serves both as head of state and head of the executive. Congress legislates and controls appropriations, while the president can veto legislation subject to constitutional override procedures. The Senate has important roles in confirming senior appointments and approving treaties, and federal courts can review the constitutionality of government action. Because the executive does not depend on day-to-day parliamentary confidence, a president may remain in office even when the opposing party controls one or both houses of Congress. This can produce negotiation and checks, but also institutional deadlock. The system therefore distributes authority rather than guaranteeing cooperation. A proper comparison with Britain should use consistent headings such as selection of the executive, tenure, legislative relationship, head of state and checks on power.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','comprehend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','cg-deeper-election-h','type','heading2','content','What makes an electoral process credible?','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend')),
    jsonb_build_object('id','cg-deeper-election-p','type','paragraph','content','An election is a process, not only polling day. Credibility depends on rules for voter registration, nomination of candidates, campaigning, access to information, polling, counting, declaration of results and resolution of disputes. Electoral bodies require legal authority and operational independence, while parties and candidates need confidence that rules will be applied consistently. Secret voting protects voters from coercion. Transparent counting and observation can strengthen trust, and accessible dispute mechanisms allow complaints to be handled through institutions rather than violence. Political freedoms, security and responsible conduct by parties and state agencies also matter. No electoral system removes political conflict completely. The key historical and civic question is whether institutions channel competition peacefully and whether citizens can choose representatives under rules regarded as legitimate. This is a stronger basis for comparison than simply labelling one country democratic and another undemocratic.','meta',jsonb_build_object('learning_layer','extend'))
  ))
)
update public.vibe_chapters c
set blocks=c.blocks || p.additions,updated_at=now()
from target t,packs p
where c.publication_id=t.id and c.number=p.number
  and not exists (select 1 from jsonb_array_elements(c.blocks) b where b->>'id'=p.sentinel);

-- Keep displayed depth/reading time truthful to the actual rendered block text.
with target as (
  select id from public.vibe_publications
  where title='Vibe History & Government Form 4' and status='published'
  order by published_at desc nulls last limit 1
), wc as (
  select c.id,
         coalesce(sum(case
           when trim(regexp_replace(coalesce(b.elem->>'content',''),'<[^>]*>','','g'))='' then 0
           else cardinality(regexp_split_to_array(trim(regexp_replace(coalesce(b.elem->>'content',''),'<[^>]*>','','g')),'\s+'))
         end),0)::int actual_words
  from public.vibe_chapters c
  join target t on t.id=c.publication_id
  cross join lateral jsonb_array_elements(c.blocks) b(elem)
  group by c.id
)
update public.vibe_chapters c
set word_count=wc.actual_words,
    reading_time_min=greatest(1,ceil(wc.actual_words/200.0)::int),
    updated_at=now()
from wc where c.id=wc.id;

-- Fail closed if a chapter unexpectedly remains shallow after this pass.
do $$
declare shallow_count integer;
begin
  select count(*) into shallow_count
  from public.vibe_chapters c
  join public.vibe_publications p on p.id=c.publication_id
  where p.title='Vibe History & Government Form 4'
    and p.status='published'
    and c.word_count < 1200;
  if shallow_count <> 0 then
    raise exception 'FORM4_HISTORY_DEPTH_REPAIR_INCOMPLETE: % chapters under 1200 rendered words', shallow_count;
  end if;
end $$;

commit;
